= Noosphere — Simple =

The '''noosphere''' is a historical idea about human thought, knowledge, and activity becoming important at the scale of the whole planet.

The concept was developed through the work of Vladimir Vernadsky, Édouard Le Roy, and Pierre Teilhard de Chardin during the early twentieth century.

They did '''not''' all mean exactly the same thing.

== Vernadsky's version ==

Vladimir Vernadsky was a biogeochemist who studied the biosphere.

He argued that living things transform Earth chemically and that human scientific activity was becoming a new geological force.

Modern evidence strongly supports the basic observation that human activity changes the planet through industry, agriculture, energy use, mining, and other processes.

But this does not prove that Earth has a mind.

== Teilhard's version ==

Pierre Teilhard de Chardin developed a more philosophical and religious theory.

He imagined human minds becoming increasingly connected and forming a noosphere that would develop toward greater consciousness and eventually an '''Omega Point'''.

Teilhard's theory mixes evolutionary ideas with Christian theology.<ref name="grumett">[https://doi.org/10.1177/0040571X19872102 Grumett, D. (2019), discussion of Teilhard's ''The Phenomenon of Man''.]</ref>

The Omega Point is not an established result of evolutionary science.

== Where did the term come from? ==

The history is complicated.

Vernadsky gave lectures in Paris about the biosphere in 1922–1923. Le Roy and Teilhard discussed related ideas. Historical scholarship indicates that Teilhard used the word in an unpublished 1925 text and Le Roy was the first to publish it.<ref name="review">[https://doi.org/10.1002/sres.2997 “What is the noosphere? Planetary superorganism, major evolutionary transition and emergence” (2023).]</ref>

Vernadsky later adapted the idea to his own scientific framework.

So the noosphere should not be credited simply to one person.

== Human influence on Earth ==

Some parts of noosphere thinking can be studied directly.

Scientists can measure:

* carbon emissions;
* land-use change;
* mining;
* biodiversity loss;
* nitrogen cycles;
* energy use;
* human-made materials.

These measurements show that humanity is a major planetary force.

Whether that should be called a “noosphere” is a conceptual choice.

== Global communication ==

The Internet allows human knowledge and communication to spread around the planet.

Some people interpret this as noospheric development.

But global connection does not mean global agreement or one mind.

The Internet also carries misinformation, conflict, surveillance, and unequal access.

== Noosphere versus global brain ==

The [[Global brain]] is a newer idea focused more directly on networks and information processing.

The noosphere is older and includes philosophical and evolutionary claims about humanity's role on Earth.

== What is established? ==

Established:

* the history of the concept;
* large-scale human environmental influence;
* global communication and knowledge networks.

Not established:

* a literal planetary sphere of thought;
* a single global consciousness;
* inevitable evolution toward an Omega Point.

